      w_x1_mpe_in_budget: "{p}: source “{s}” — the maximum permissible error (MPE) of the instrument under calibration is included in the uncertainty budget (share {v} %).",
      w_x2_certificate_divisor: "{p}: “{s}” — a certificate source with a normal distribution is divided by {v}.",
      w_x2_certificate_rect: "{p}: “{s}” — a certificate source is entered with a rectangular distribution (√3).",
      w_x3_hysteresis_double: "{p}: hysteresis is counted twice — source “{s}” was added manually ({v}).",
      w_x4_correlation_ignored: "{p}: the paired observations give {v}, but correlation is not taken into account.",
      w_x4_shared_reference: "{p}: the same source “{s}” is used for both inputs, but the covariance is 0.",
      w_x5_resolution_divisor: "{p}: the divisor of the resolution source “{s}” looks wrong ({v}).",
      w_x5_resolution_distribution: "{p}: the resolution source “{s}” is entered with the “{v}” distribution.",
      w_x5_resolution_rect: "{p}: the resolution source “{s}” is entered with a rectangular distribution (a/√3).",
      w_x5_unusual_divisor: "{p}: unusual divisor in source “{s}”: {v}.",
      w_x6_source_units_differ: "{p}: the unit of source “{s}” differs ({v}) while the sensitivity multiplier is 1.",
      w_x7_k2_low_dof: "A fixed k = 2 is used, but {v} — the effective degrees of freedom are low.",
      w_x7_k2_few_observations: "A fixed k = 2 is used, but the Type A share is large and the observations are few ({p}: {v}).",
      w_x8_zero_s_no_resolution: "{p}: all readings are identical (s = 0) and there is no resolution source for this input.",
      w_x9_too_many_digits: "U is reported with more than two significant digits: {v}.",
      w_x9_decimals_mismatch: "The decimal places of the result and of U do not match in the report: {v}.",
      w_x10_no_drift: "There is a reference standard source ({s}) but no drift or long-term stability source.",
      w_dominant_source: "One source dominates: “{s}” — {v} of u_c².",
      w_n_lt3: "{p}: too few observations ({v}).",
      w_invalid_nu: "{p}: invalid degrees of freedom for “{s}” ({v}).",
      w_grubbs_outlier: "{p}: possible outlier by the Grubbs test ({v}).",
      w_model_kind_unknown: "An MPE is given but the model type is not specified — no statement of conformity was issued.",
      w_model_value_no_nominal: "The model type is “quantity value” but no nominal value is given — no statement of conformity was issued.",

      why_x1_mpe_in_budget: "In a calibration the error of the instrument is the measurand itself. The MPE is a requirement on the instrument: the result is compared with it. If it is put in the budget, U repeats the instrument specification instead of describing the quality of the measurement.",
      why_x2_certificate_divisor: "A certificate states the expanded uncertainty U with a coverage factor k; the standard uncertainty is u = U / k. Any other divisor distorts u.",
      why_x2_certificate_rect: "A rectangular distribution is appropriate only when the document states limits (±a). If the certificate states U (k), the divisor is k, not √3.",
      why_x3_hysteresis_double: "An effect must not be counted twice: the difference between directions is already contained in the scatter of the observations or in the automatic contribution of the ↑/↓ mode.",
      why_x4_correlation_ignored: "For correlated inputs u_c² contains the term 2·c_i·c_j·u(x_i, x_j). Omitting it under- or overestimates u_c.",
      why_x4_shared_reference: "If two inputs depend on the same standard or certificate they are correlated; in difference and ratio models this changes the result noticeably.",
      why_x5_resolution_divisor: "For a resolution δ the width of the distribution is δ: u = δ/(2√3) = 0.29·δ. Dividing the full δ by √3 doubles u; dividing δ/2 by 2√3 halves it.",
      why_x5_resolution_distribution: "Within the resolution all values are equally probable — a rectangular distribution.",
      why_x5_resolution_rect: "This is correct if the half-width entered is a = δ/2. If the full step δ was entered, u is twice too large.",
      why_x5_unusual_divisor: "Certificates usually state k = 2 (95 %) or k = 3; k = 1 means a standard uncertainty. A value outside this range is most often an input error.",
      why_x6_source_units_differ: "The contribution c·u(x) must be in the unit of the result. With another unit (or scale: mK and K) the contribution is wrong by a large factor.",
      why_x7_k2_low_dof: "k = 2 gives about 95 % coverage only if a normal distribution can be attributed to the result and u(y) is sufficiently reliable. With low ν_eff the actual coverage is below 95 %.",
      why_x7_k2_few_observations: "The reliability criterion is met when no Type A evaluation is based on fewer than ten observations; otherwise k is taken from ν_eff.",
      why_zero_u: "An uncertainty cannot be zero: even with identical readings the contributions of resolution and of the reference standard remain.",
      why_below_cmc: "The uncertainty stated on a certificate must not be smaller than the CMC in the scope of accreditation. The program does not replace U automatically.",
      why_zero_s: "Identical readings do not mean zero repeatability — it is smaller than the resolution. The resolution contribution is in the budget.",
      why_x8_zero_s_no_resolution: "Even with identical readings the indication is read in finite steps: the resolution contribution u = δ/(2√3) is needed.",
      why_x9_too_many_digits: "An uncertainty is stated to at most two significant digits; more digits suggest an accuracy that does not exist.",
      why_x9_decimals_mismatch: "The result is rounded to the last digit of the uncertainty.",
      why_x10_no_drift: "The value of the standard may have changed since its last calibration; in the EA-4/02 examples this is a separate source.",
      why_dominant_source: "With one dominant contribution the distribution of the result is close to that of the contribution; a k based on a normal distribution (2 or t) may give excess coverage (k = 1.65 at 95 % for a rectangular one).",
      why_n_lt3: "With n = 2 the standard deviation itself has an uncertainty of about 76 % — such a Type A estimate is unreliable.",
      why_n_small: "With few observations s itself has a large uncertainty (about 52 % for n = 3).",
      why_invalid_nu: "Degrees of freedom are a positive number. The program treats an invalid value as ∞ — ν_eff and k may come out optimistic.",
      why_grubbs_outlier: "An outlier distorts the mean and s. The test is only a hint: the value is not removed automatically.",
      why_model_kind_unknown: "The program does not assume that the result is an error: if the result is a quantity value (e.g. a density), comparing |y| with the MPE is meaningless.",
      why_model_value_no_nominal: "For a quantity value |Y − Y_nom| is compared with the MPE — a nominal value is needed.",
      why_unit_budget: "The contribution c·u(x) must be in the unit of the result.",
      why_unit_output: "The stated unit of the result does not match the dimension that follows from the formula.",
      why_unit_affine: "°C is an offset scale: only temperature differences or kelvins may be multiplied or divided.",
      why_unit_pow: "With a variable exponent the dimension cannot be checked.",
      why_x11_double_counting: "If one factor is counted twice, u_c is overestimated. The contribution was kept in the calculation — the decision is the laboratory's.",
      why_x12_empirical_bias: "For an empirical (method-defined) measurand the method bias is zero by definition; a bias is included only if the reference value was obtained by the same method.",
      why_x12_bias_excluded: "The reference value was obtained by another method — this is method bias, which is not included for an empirical measurand.",

      fix_x1_mpe_in_budget: "Remove this source from the budget; enter the MPE in the “Conformity assessment” field. Only the repeatability and resolution of the instrument stay in the budget. If this is the error of the reference standard (measuring equipment) — clarify its name or accept with a justification.",
